-6b6c0a4f5d77" :question/math-academy-id "q-141612" :question/is-example false :question/problem "Complete the square on the quadratic expression ${y}^{2}-4y+10.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 2y+10\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${2}^{2},$ writing the constant term $10$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 2y+{2}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{2}^{2}+10\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y-2{)}^{2}-{2}^{2}+10\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y-2{)}^{2}+6.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-141612/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "08c6d8d6-3d86-5074-bf99-0cb744c9412a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-141612"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-235868/field-1/c92a5ced6c9622c01e21ae7e08fb0538ea56aab9fb51b31b9940d156de39ef32" :answer/id #uuid "7120ee9e-6161-5b50-833f-95ed8c761ff9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-235868/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "a24459a0-9d04-5b69-9e54-163c9dd8e320" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-235868/field-1/c92a5ced6c9622c01e21ae7e08fb0538ea56aab9fb51b31b9940d156de39ef32" :answer-field/choices ["q-235868/field-1/c92a5ced6c9622c01e21ae7e08fb0538ea56aab9fb51b31b9940d156de39ef32"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-235868/field-2/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "0a027bc2-5783-5794-a2b5-759cc27f74fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-235868/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "b23ac193-102a-5a4f-aa9f-d4ee1c56d332" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-235868/field-2/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer-field/choices ["q-235868/field-2/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-235868" :question/id #uuid "b4e54afd-2abe-5a88-9ee5-e6a69b0457db" :question/math-academy-id "q-235868" :question/is-example false :question/problem "Rewrite the following expression as the sum of a perfect square and a constant.\n\n$({x}^{2}-8x+16)+5=(${{field-1}}${)}^{2}\\,+$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2ax+{a}^{2}=(x-a{)}^{2}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-8x+16 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 4x+{4}^{2} & = \\\\ (x-4{)}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-8x+16)+5 & = \\\\ (x-4{)}^{2}+5 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-235868/field-1" "q-235868/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf9b812d-0dbf-5a67-9d45-3cc5e373b2b1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-235868"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-250517/field-1/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "b868bb8d-6c3c-51cf-9698-cf3a7a2b66d1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-250517/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "9becf198-6dc5-5b25-a7ce-56ad390e4099" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-250517/field-1/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer-field/choices ["q-250517/field-1/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-250517/field-2/e0e0a3ac4fe95f021c8f8aece7092270d36ad30c356733db1e628dc4f0b1d434" :answer/id #uuid "37b2f258-6637-50b4-a215-a239625f81d6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-250517/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "9b642f8e-a43d-5b26-834b-489394c49391" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-250517/field-2/e0e0a3ac4fe95f021c8f8aece7092270d36ad30c356733db1e628dc4f0b1d434" :answer-field/choices ["q-250517/field-2/e0e0a3ac4fe95f021c8f8aece7092270d36ad30c356733db1e628dc4f0b1d434"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-250517" :question/id #uuid "cf9fb661-19c7-51e6-ac0f-2fa500a29101" :question/math-academy-id "q-250517" :question/is-example false :question/problem "If the expression ${x}^{2}-10x$ is written as $(x-p{)}^{2}+q$, then what are the values of $p$ and $q?$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}},\n$\\,q=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 5x+0\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${5}^{2},$ writing the constant term $0$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 5x+{5}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{5}^{2}+0\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-5{)}^{2}-{5}^{2}+0\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-5{)}^{2}-25\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=5$ and $q=-25.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-250517/field-1" "q-250517/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "08c6d8d6-3d86-5074-bf99-0cb744c9412a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-250517"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
